.TH IDO 1 "2026-10-14" "ido 0.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ido \- a queue of commands an agent asked you to run
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ido
.br
.B ido queue
.RB [ \-m
.IR text ]
.RB [ \-\-group
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-step
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-needs
.IR id ]...
.RB [ \-\-cwd
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run\-of
.IR arg ]...
.B \-\-
.I command
.RI [ arg ...]
.br
.B ido add
.RI [ path ]
.br
.B ido list
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B ido run
.RI { id ...
|
.B \-\-group
.I name
|
.BR \-\-all }
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-animation ]
.br
.B ido drop
.RI { id ...
|
.B \-\-all
|
.BR \-\-stale }
.br
.B ido log
.RB [ \-n
.IR count ]
.br
.B ido celebrate
.RB [ \-\-no\-animation ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
An agent queues the commands it cannot or should not run.
You run
.BR ido ,
see the queue, and choose what to run, in your own terminal.
Nothing runs without a keystroke from you.
.PP
.B ido
holds no privilege.
It gates on consequence, not on uid: a queued
.B sudo
command asks for your password as it always would, and a cloud command that
needs no root is shown with the profile, region, or project it would act on.
.PP
Every field in the queue was written by the agent.
Before anything is shown, escape sequences, control characters,
bidirectional overrides, and invisible characters are removed, and a command
name containing non-ASCII characters is flagged as a possible lookalike.
The command is shown token by token and executed exactly as stored, with no
shell involved.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ido
On a terminal, opens the picker.
Without a terminal on both standard input and standard output, behaves as
.B ido list
and runs nothing.
.TP
.B queue
Buffers one command and prints its id.
Never waits.
The command is a real argument vector after
.BR \-\- ;
a shell string is not accepted.
The same command, directory, group, and step already queued is not queued
again.
A
.B \-\-needs
id must already be queued, so a dependency cycle cannot be written.
.TP
.B add
Writes the agent instructions between
.B <!\-\- ido:begin
and
.B <!\-\- ido:end \-\->
markers in
.BR AGENTS.md ,
or in the named
.BR CLAUDE.md ,
.BR GEMINI.md ,
.BR .cursorrules ,
or
.BR copilot\-instructions.md .
A second run updates the block; text outside the markers is kept byte for
byte.
The first change backs the original up to
.IB file .ido\-backup .
A relative path may not leave the current directory, and a symlink is
refused.
.TP
.B list
Prints the queue.
.B \-\-json
prints the exact argv of each entry, as ASCII JSON.
.TP
.B run
Runs entries in order and stops at the first failure.
On a terminal, shows what will run and asks you to type
.BR run ;
.B \-\-yes
skips that and is refused on a terminal.
Without a terminal,
.B \-\-yes
is required.
.B \-\-all
leaves failed entries for an explicit retry by id.
.TP
.B drop
Discards entries.
Dropping an entry satisfies whatever depends on it.
.TP
.B log
Shows what was run and how it exited, from a hash-chained log that
.B xudo audit
can verify.
.TP
.B celebrate
Plays a short abstract animation and prints
.BR "ido works." .
The same binary installed as
.B willyoumarryme
does the same, so that name works as a queued command.
.SH THE PICKER
.TP
.BR \(ua " " \(da " " j " " k
Move.
.TP
.B space
Select or unselect.
A blocked or halted entry cannot be selected, and the reason is shown.
.TP
.B g
Select every step of the highlighted entry's group.
.TP
.B enter
Run the selection.
With nothing selected, nothing runs.
More than one entry, or any entry that elevates, drives a cloud CLI, or has a
lookalike command name, needs the word
.B run
typed out.
.TP
.B d
Drop the selection, or the highlighted entry, after
.BR y .
.TP
.B p
Show the highlighted entry token by token, with its directory.
A
.B \-\-dry\-run\-of
preview is shown, never run automatically.
.TP
.BR q ", " esc
Quit.
.SH GROUPS
Steps of a group run one at a time, in step order.
A step is blocked until the step before it has exited 0.
If a step fails, the rest of its group is halted; retry the failed step by id,
or drop it to unblock the rest.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything asked for happened.
.TP
.B 1
A command failed, an entry was not runnable when its turn came, the
confirmation was not given, or a file could not be read or written.
.TP
.B 2
The request was refused before anything ran.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR XDG_STATE_HOME ", " XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the queue and its lock live; see
.BR ido\-queue (5).
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables colour.
.TP
.B IDO_NO_ANIMATION
Disables the celebration animation.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/ido/queue.jsonl
The queue.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/ido/log.jsonl
The run log.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ido\-queue (5),
.BR xudo (1)
